  <p>
    NixOS uses the repository's flake instead of the one-line installer; the snippet is in the
    <a href={`${REPO}#cài`}>README</a>. CI builds it, runs the tests and loads the addon into fcitx5 on
    Xvfb, but nobody has tried it on a real NixOS machine yet, nor on Wayland, GNOME or aarch64.
  </p>

  <h2 id="known-issues">Known issues</h2>
  <p>
    Not fixed: SDL games not running on X11 ignore forwarded keys and get text without tone marks, and the
    snap build of VS Code types raw Telex; the workaround routes it through XIM, where about one sentence
    in ten gets a wrong tone mark. In Preedit mode on GNOME Wayland, Chrome and Chromium-based apps
    running natively on Wayland lose the word being typed on a mouse click until the distribution
    ships fcitx5 5.1.20. Telex is the method used and tested daily; VNI and the other methods
    work but have not been tested closely.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap (in Vietnamese)</a> has no numbered version after 1.7.0 yet; its
    “Sau đó” list keeps the planned work in order, ending with reworked emoji insertion and merging
    spell checking into automatic restoration of invalid words. The roadmap gives no release dates, and
    the order may change.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ngó Sen is built for fcitx5, which runs on GNOME, KDE and Xfce as well as Sway and Hyprland, has
    plenty of features and is light on RAM. <a href="https://github.com/ngosen/ibus-ngosen">Ngó Sen
    for GNOME</a> runs on GNOME’s built-in IBus instead; it is simpler and has fewer features than the
    fcitx5 edition. Source, releases and the
    changelog are at
    <a href={REPO}>github.com/ngosen/ngosen</a>.
  </p>
